[
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev05-01",
    "question": "<p>Prove by induction that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(n)=2^{n+2}+3^{2n+1}\"></span> is divisible by 7 for all positive integers n. <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Increasing <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span> by one multiplies the first power by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\"></span> and the second by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3^2=9\"></span>. Subtracting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2f(k)\"></span> cancels the first power exactly. The second coefficient is then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"9-2=7\"></span>, which is how to spot the useful multiple of the hypothesis.</p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(1)=8+27=35=5\\cdot7\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k)=7m\"></span> for an integer <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=2\\cdot2^{k+2}+9\\cdot3^{2k+1}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Subtracting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2f(k)\"></span> leaves <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"7\\cdot3^{2k+1}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=7(2m+3^{2k+1})\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The base case and inductive implication prove divisibility by 7 for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 6 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev05-01-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2018 · Q8, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev05-02",
    "question": "<p>(ii) Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\in\\mathbb Z^+\"></span>,</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(n)=3^{3n-2}+2^{3n+1}\"></div><p>is divisible by 19. <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Both exponents increase by three, giving multipliers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3^3=27\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2^3=8\"></span>. Their difference is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"19\"></span>. Extracting either eight or twenty-seven copies of the old expression will therefore leave an integer multiple of nineteen.</p>",
      "<p>(ii) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(1)=3+16=19\"></span>, a multiple of 19. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(k)=19m\"></span> for an integer <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm f(k+1)&amp;=27\\,3^{3k-2}+8\\,2^{3k+1}\\\\\n &amp;=8\\bigl(3^{3k-2}+2^{3k+1}\\bigr)+19\\,3^{3k-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\"></div>",
      "<p>Expanding at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> and isolating <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(k)\"></span> earn one mark each. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(k+1)=19(8m+3^{3k-2})\"></span> is divisible by 19. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The base case and inductive implication establish the result for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Equivalently, subtract <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"27\\mathrm f(k)\"></span> to obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-19\\,2^{3k+1}\"></span>.</p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 6 marks.</p>",
      "<p>Count only the retained parts. Any original whole-question total on the extract belongs to the complete exam question.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev05-02-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2017 edited · Q9, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev05-02-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2017 edited · Q9, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev05-03",
    "question": "<p>(i) Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span></p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\frac{2r+1}{r^{2}(r+1)^{2}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(ii) A sequence of positive rational numbers is defined by</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"u_{1} = 3\"></div><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"u_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{3} u_{n} + \\frac{8}{9}, \\quad n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></div><p>Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span></p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"u_{n} = 5 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{n} + \\frac{4}{3}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>For the sum, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2k+3=(k+2)^2-(k+1)^2\"></span>, so the next fraction cancels the old residual square. For the recurrence, the constant term <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4/3\"></span> reproduces itself since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(4/3)/3+8/9=4/3\"></span>. Only the decaying power changes from one index to the next.</p>",
      "<p>(i) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> both sides are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3/4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_k=1-(k+1)^{-2}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Add <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2k+3)/[(k+1)^2(k+2)^2]\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Over a common denominator the subtracted numerator is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+2)^2-(2k+3)=(k+1)^2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{k+1}=1-(k+2)^{-2}\"></span>. Induction establishes the identity for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(ii) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> the formula gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5/3+4/3=3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The optional check at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=2\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"17/9\"></span> by either formula.\nAssume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_k=5(1/3)^k+4/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The recurrence gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}=5(1/3)^{k+1}+4/9+8/9\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5(1/3)^{k+1}+4/3\"></span>, the required next term. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The result follows by induction. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
